## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props or state change. For primitives (`string`, `number`), change means a different value. For objects and functions, change means a different **reference** — even if the contents are identical.

```jsx
// Every render creates a NEW object reference → child always re-renders
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Every render creates a NEW function reference → same problem
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

JavaScript's `{}` and `() => {}` always produce new references on each call, so React sees "new prop" every time the parent renders.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | Memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | A **component** — skips re-render if props haven't changed | Child is pure and expensive, and its props are stable |
| `useMemo` | A **computed value** — recalculates only when deps change | Expensive derivation (sorting, filtering large arrays) |
| `useCallback` | A **function reference** — returns the same ref across renders | Passing callbacks to memoized children or as effect deps |

**`React.memo` is useless without stable references.** If you wrap a child in `memo` but still pass inline objects/functions, the memo check will always fail.

```jsx
// useCallback stabilizes the reference
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);

// useMemo stabilizes an object
const style = useMemo(() => ({ color: theme.primary }), [theme.primary]);

// React.memo makes the child opt into shallow-prop comparison
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, style }) => <div style={style} onClick={onClick} />);
```

**Don't over-memoize.** For cheap components or rarely-updated props, memoization adds overhead (cache comparison cost) with no benefit. Profile first.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer of a context re-renders whenever the context **value** changes. The trap: passing an inline object as the value.

```jsx
// BAD — new object reference every render → ALL consumers re-render
<AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, logout }}>

// GOOD — memoize the value object
const authValue = useMemo(() => ({ user, logout }), [user, logout]);
<AuthContext.Provider value={authValue}>
```

A second pitfall: **one giant context** means unrelated consumers re-render when any slice of state changes. Split contexts by update frequency — e.g., separate `UserContext` from `ThemeContext`.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent:

```jsx
function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const items = fetchedItems; // stable array from outside

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      {/* inline filter + inline callback = new refs every render */}
      <ExpensiveList
        items={items.filter(i => i.active)}
        onSelect={item => console.log(item)}
      />
    </>
  );
}
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` or `onSelect` actually change:

```jsx
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  return items.map(i => <Row key={i.id} item={i} onSelect={onSelect} />);
});

function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const items = fetchedItems;

  const activeItems = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.active),
    [items]           // only recomputes when items array changes
  );

  const handleSelect = useCallback(
    item => console.log(item),
    []                // no deps — function identity is stable forever
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={activeItems} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}
```

Typing in the input now only re-renders `Dashboard` — `ExpensiveList` is skipped entirely because both `activeItems` and `handleSelect` keep their references stable.
